Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The product rule gives $\frac{d}{dx}(xy)=y+x\frac{dy}{dx}$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y + 1+\left(x - 1\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\
\left(x - 1\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\left(y + 1\right) \\
\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\frac{y + 1}{x - 1}
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope at the given point:

$$
m_{\mathrm{t}}=-\frac{-3}{-5}=- \frac{3}{5}
$$

The normal slope is the negative reciprocal:

$$
m=-\frac{1}{m_{\mathrm{t}}}=-\frac{1}{- \frac{3}{5}}=\frac{5}{3}
$$

Use the given point and this slope:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(-4\right)&=\frac{5}{3}\left(x-\left(-4\right)\right) \\
y&=\frac{5}{3}\left(x-\left(-4\right)\right)+\left(-4\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$. Substitute and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=\frac{5}{3}\left(0-\left(-4\right)\right)+\left(-4\right) \\
y&=\frac{8}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore $P=\left(0,\frac{8}{3}\right)$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
6 x+\left(- 8 y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\
\left(- 8 y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\left(6 x\right) \\
\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\frac{6 x}{- 8 y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope at the given point:

$$
m_{\mathrm{t}}=-\frac{6}{-8}=\frac{3}{4}
$$

The normal slope is the negative reciprocal:

$$
m=-\frac{1}{m_{\mathrm{t}}}=-\frac{1}{\frac{3}{4}}=- \frac{4}{3}
$$

Use the given point and this slope:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(1\right)&=- \frac{4}{3}\left(x-\left(1\right)\right) \\
y&=- \frac{4}{3}\left(x-\left(1\right)\right)+\left(1\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$. Substitute and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=- \frac{4}{3}\left(0-\left(1\right)\right)+\left(1\right) \\
y&=\frac{7}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore $P=\left(0,\frac{7}{3}\right)$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
6 x + 4+\left(4 y + 4\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\
\left(4 y + 4\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\left(6 x + 4\right) \\
\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\frac{6 x + 4}{4 y + 4}
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope at the given point:

$$
m_{\mathrm{t}}=-\frac{22}{4}=- \frac{11}{2}
$$

The normal slope is the negative reciprocal:

$$
m=-\frac{1}{m_{\mathrm{t}}}=-\frac{1}{- \frac{11}{2}}=\frac{2}{11}
$$

Use the given point and this slope:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(0\right)&=\frac{2}{11}\left(x-\left(3\right)\right) \\
y&=\frac{2}{11}\left(x-\left(3\right)\right)+\left(0\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$. Substitute and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=\frac{2}{11}\left(0-\left(3\right)\right)+\left(0\right) \\
y&=- \frac{6}{11}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore $P=\left(0,- \frac{6}{11}\right)$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The product rule gives $\frac{d}{dx}(xy)=y+x\frac{dy}{dx}$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
4 x + y+\left(x + 4 y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\
\left(x + 4 y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\left(4 x + y\right) \\
\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\frac{4 x + y}{x + 4 y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope at the given point:

$$
m_{\mathrm{t}}=-\frac{-12}{-18}=- \frac{2}{3}
$$

The normal slope is the negative reciprocal:

$$
m=-\frac{1}{m_{\mathrm{t}}}=-\frac{1}{- \frac{2}{3}}=\frac{3}{2}
$$

Use the given point and this slope:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(-4\right)&=\frac{3}{2}\left(x-\left(-2\right)\right) \\
y&=\frac{3}{2}\left(x-\left(-2\right)\right)+\left(-4\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Substitute and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=\frac{3}{2}\left(x-\left(-2\right)\right)+\left(-4\right) \\
x-\left(-2\right)&=-\frac{-4}{\frac{3}{2}} \\
x&=\frac{2}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore $P=\left(\frac{2}{3},0\right)$.
